content:
In this walkthrough you set up a block theme called my-theme and give it a first block: a teaser with a title and a text that content editors type directly into the editor canvas, and a switch in the inspector that highlights it. Along the way you see every file a consumer of the framework needs and why it is there.

Plugins and child themes with code of their own are set up the same way. Boot and modes, Plugins and Child themes describe what differs.

What you need

You need PHP 8.5, Composer and Node 24. The simplest way to get them is wp-dev-env: it runs them in a container together with a local WordPress, so you only need Docker on your machine.

The files of the theme

When you're done, the theme looks like this:

my-theme/
├── composer.json
├── functions.php
├── package.json
├── style.css
├── theme.json
├── templates/index.html
├── webpack.config.js
├── assets/
│   └── styles.css
└── src/
    └── Block/Teaser/
        ├── Teaser.php
        ├── TeaserAttributes.php
        └── Teaser.twig

style.css, theme.json and templates/index.html are the same as in any block theme. composer.json and functions.php bring in the framework and start it. package.json and webpack.config.js build the JavaScript for the editor and the stylesheet. Your own code lives in src/; the stylesheet lives in assets/, the directory for everything that belongs to no PHP class.

composer.json

Composer installs the framework and tells it where your classes are. The framework comes from Packagist like any other package; the lock file pins the exact version. The PSR-4 namespace Acme\MyTheme\ maps to src/, and every class in there is something the framework can use – a block, a hook, a service.

{
    "name": "acme/my-theme",
    "type": "wordpress-theme",
    "license": "GPL-2.0-or-later",
    "require": {
        "php": ">=8.5",
        "saij/wp-app-framework": "^0.9"
    },
    "autoload": {
        "psr-4": { "Acme\\MyTheme\\": "src/" }
    },
    "scripts": {
        "build-manifest": "wp-app-framework build",
        "release": "wp-app-framework release"
    },
    "extra": {
        "wp-app-framework": {
            "namespace": "my-theme"
        },
        "strauss": {
            "target_directory": "vendor-prefixed",
            "namespace_prefix": "Acme\\MyTheme\\Vendor\\",
            "classmap_prefix": "Acme_MyTheme_Vendor_",
            "include_root_autoload": true,
            "update_call_sites": ["src", "functions.php"],
            "delete_vendor_packages": false
        }
    },
    "config": {
        "platform": { "php": "8.5" }
    }
}

A few entries deserve a closer look:

type tells the framework's build what kind of project this is. wordpress-theme covers themes and child themes, wordpress-plugin plugins.

The two scripts give you short commands for the framework's tools: composer build-manifest checks your code, and composer release builds a ZIP for production.

extra.wp-app-framework.namespace names your blocks, REST routes and settings: the block teaser below is registered as my-theme/teaser. Choose it once; content refers to it (Concepts explains why it is separate from the directory name).

extra.strauss configures the prefixing of the release build. A release renames the framework and all dependencies into your own namespace (here Acme\MyTheme\Vendor\), so they cannot collide with other themes or plugins. Release explains each value.

style.css

The theme header works as usual. Version: is the version the release build puts into the ZIP's file name:

/*
Theme Name: My Theme
Version: 0.1.0
Requires at least: 7.1
Requires PHP: 8.5
Text Domain: my-theme
*/

functions.php

functions.php loads Composer's autoloader and boots the framework. A release ships the prefixed dependencies in vendor-prefixed/ instead of vendor/, so the file picks whichever exists:

<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

// Release builds ship prefixed dependencies in vendor-prefixed/; source checkouts use Composer's vendor/.
require is_file(__DIR__ . '/vendor-prefixed/autoload.php')
    ? __DIR__ . '/vendor-prefixed/autoload.php'
    : __DIR__ . '/vendor/autoload.php';

Saij\WpAppFramework\Theme::boot();

That is the only call into the framework you write by hand. Everything else – blocks, hooks, routes – the framework finds through the attributes on your classes.

package.json and webpack.config.js

The block editor needs JavaScript: the framework's editor runtime, which makes your blocks editable, and any editor scripts of your own. Blocks with front-end scripts need them built as well. The framework ships only the sources of the runtime. Your theme builds them together with its own scripts and its stylesheet, using @wordpress/scripts and a webpack helper from the framework (see Editor).

{
	"name": "my-theme",
	"private": true,
	"scripts": { "build": "wp-scripts build", "start": "wp-scripts start" },
	"devDependencies": {
		"@saij/wp-app-framework": "file:vendor/saij/wp-app-framework",
		"@tailwindcss/webpack": "^4.3.3",
		"@wordpress/scripts": "^36.1.0",
		"tailwindcss": "^4.3.3"
	}
}

The helper needs the slug of the theme, which is its directory name:

module.exports = require( './vendor/saij/wp-app-framework/webpack.config.js' )( {
	slug: 'my-theme',
	root: __dirname,
} );

assets/styles.css

Blocks are styled with Tailwind CSS. If the theme has an assets/styles.css, the build compiles it with Tailwind, and the framework loads it on the front end and in the editor canvas. The stylesheet maps Tailwind's tokens to the values of theme.json. Here bg-primary uses the color primary, which theme.json defines:

@layer theme, base, components, utilities;
@import "tailwindcss/theme.css" layer(theme);
@import "tailwindcss/utilities.css" layer(utilities) source(none);
@source "../src/**/*.twig";

@theme inline {
	--color-primary: var(--wp--preset--color--primary);
}

Styles explains each line.

A first block

A block consists of three parts: a class that describes the block and renders it, a class that holds its attributes, and a Twig template with the markup. They sit next to each other in src/Block/Teaser/.

The block class carries #[Block] with the block's name, title, category and icon. Its constructor asks for the TemplateRenderer, which the framework provides, and render() passes the attributes to the template:

<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Acme\MyTheme\Block\Teaser;

use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\Block;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\BlockCategory;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Twig\TemplateRenderer;

#[Block('teaser', title: 'Teaser', category: BlockCategory::Text, icon: 'text')]
final readonly class Teaser
{
    public function __construct(private TemplateRenderer $renderer) {}

    public function render(TeaserAttributes $attrs): string
    {
        return $this->renderer->render('Block/Teaser/Teaser.twig', ['attrs' => $attrs]);
    }
}

The attribute class lists what content editors can set. Each constructor parameter becomes a block attribute. #[PlainText] and #[RichText] mark text that is edited directly in the canvas; the plain bool gets a toggle in the block inspector automatically.

<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Acme\MyTheme\Block\Teaser;

use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\PlainText;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\RichText;

final readonly class TeaserAttributes
{
    public function __construct(
        #[PlainText] public string $title = '',
        #[RichText(allowedFormats: ['core/bold', 'core/italic'])] public string $text = '',
        public bool $highlighted = false,
    ) {}
}

The template renders the markup. block_wrapper_attributes() goes on the root element and adds the classes WordPress needs. field('title') marks the element that shows the title, so that the editor can make it editable; on the front end it prints nothing.

<section {{ block_wrapper_attributes({class: attrs.highlighted ? 'p-4 bg-primary' : 'p-4'}) }}>
    <h2{{ field('title') }}>{{ attrs.title }}</h2>
    <div{{ field('text') }}>{{ attrs.text|raw }}</div>
</section>

The template switches between two complete class names instead of building one from the attribute. That is deliberate: Tailwind finds class names by reading the templates, so it only generates classes that appear there in full.

Blocks covers all of this in depth.

Build and run

Install the dependencies and build the assets. Run composer install first, because the file: dependency in package.json points into vendor/:

composer install
npm install
npm run build             # editor runtime, editor and front-end scripts and stylesheet into build/
composer build-manifest   # checks attributes and services; writes nothing

composer build-manifest is optional here, but useful: it checks your attributes and services and reports problems with class and parameter names.

Now activate the theme in WordPress and add the teaser to a page. You don't need to build anything else while you develop. The framework builds its manifest itself and caches it in wp-content/cache/my-theme/ (see Boot and modes), and npm run start rebuilds the editor scripts and the stylesheet whenever you change them.

When you are ready to ship the theme, Release shows how to build a ZIP that runs in any WordPress installation.

A theme ships more than code: a logo, icons, placeholder images, a font, a PDF to download. These files live in the assets/ directory of the theme or plugin, next to src/. The framework gives each a URL that changes with its content, prints images with their size and in smaller versions and modern formats, inlines SVG icons, preloads fonts, imports files into the media library and lets a child theme replace a file without code.

How it works

Every file in assets/ is a static asset, except hidden files and the sources of the script build – .js, .jsx, .ts, .tsx, .css and .scss (see Concepts). You name an asset by its path below assets/: images/logo.svg, fonts/inter.woff2.

The build lists the assets in the manifest, each with a short hash of its content and, for images, its width and height. At run time the framework reads everything it needs from there and never touches the file system for an asset of the instance. The hash goes into the URL as ?ver=…, so browsers and CDNs load a changed file anew while they cache an unchanged one for as long as they like. In development mode, a new, removed or changed file rebuilds the manifest on the next request.

In templates

Three Twig functions cover the usual cases. asset_url() returns the URL of any asset, for a link or a <video>:

<a href="{{ asset_url('docs/price-list.pdf') }}">Price list</a>
<video src="{{ asset_url('videos/intro.mp4') }}" muted autoplay></video>

asset_image() prints an <img> element. It sets width and height from the manifest, so the page does not jump while the image loads, and decoding="async". The alternative text is a required argument; pass '' for a decorative image. Further attributes follow as a map:

{{ asset_image('images/logo.svg', site_name, {class: 'h-10 w-auto', loading: 'lazy'}) }}

asset_svg() puts the markup of an SVG image into the page, so CSS can style it – an icon that takes the color of the text, for example. It drops the XML declaration and adds the given attributes to the root element; classes are added to those the file has:

<button class="menu-toggle">
    {{ asset_svg('icons/menu.svg', {class: 'size-6 fill-current', 'aria-hidden': 'true'}) }}
    Menu
</button>

Prefer asset_svg() over Twig's own source(): it checks that the file is an SVG image of the instance and changes its root element without string operations.

Responsive images and formats

A large photo should not reach a phone at full size. With widths and sizes, asset_image() adds a srcset of smaller versions, and the browser picks the one that fits. With formats, it wraps the image in a <picture> with a <source> per format, so browsers that understand AVIF or WebP load the smaller file:

{{ asset_image('images/hero.jpg', 'Our team', {
    widths: [640, 1280, 1920],
    sizes: '(min-width: 1200px) 1200px, 100vw',
    formats: ['avif', 'webp'],
    class: 'w-full h-auto',
}) }}

The variants are created by the build, before any template renders. The build therefore reads the templates in src/ and looks for calls of asset_image() with widths or formats: the name of the image, widths and formats must be written as literals in the call, and sizes must be there. Each variant is a file named after the image, the hash of its content, its width and format, so its URL never needs a version. A width larger than the image is left out, and the build says so. The original image stays the largest candidate of the srcset.

The build writes the variants with the PHP extension Imagick, or with GD where Imagick cannot write a format. In development mode that is the PHP of your web server, which writes them to wp-content/cache/{slug}/assets/ when the manifest is rebuilt. The release build runs on the command line and writes them to generated/assets/ of the release. If neither extension can write a requested format, the build fails and names the format and what to install. Only JPEG, PNG, WebP and AVIF images have variants; an image of an override or of the child theme is printed without them.

Preloading fonts

A font the stylesheet needs early, such as the font of the headings, should start loading with the page instead of after the stylesheet. Mark its @font-face in assets/styles.css with a comment:

@font-face {
	/* wp-app-framework: preload */
	font-family: "Inter";
	src: url("./fonts/inter-var.woff2") format("woff2");
	font-display: swap;
}

The webpack helper finds the marked rules, takes the first woff2 file of each and lists its built file in build/styles.preload.php. The framework adds a <link rel="preload" as="font" crossorigin> for each to the head of the front end, through WordPress' filter wp_preload_resources. The preload uses the same URL as the built stylesheet, so the browser loads the font once. Fonts declared in theme.json cannot be marked; preload only fonts the first view of the page needs.

In the media library

Some content needs an attachment rather than a file of the theme: an #[Image] field stores the ID of an attachment, and so does content a theme creates on activation. The service MediaLibrary imports an asset into the media library and returns the ID of its attachment:

use Saij\WpAppFramework\Asset\MediaLibrary;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Lifecycle\OnActivation;

final readonly class StarterContent
{
    public function __construct(private MediaLibrary $media) {}

    #[OnActivation]
    public function create(): void
    {
        $image = $this->media->import('images/hero.jpg');
        // … create a page whose hero block uses $image
    }
}

The import is idempotent per site: an asset that was imported with the same content returns its attachment. A changed file becomes a new attachment, and the old one stays, because content may still use it. WordPress creates the intermediate sizes as for any upload. Imported attachments stay in the media library when the theme or plugin is removed, like all attachments (see Activation and uninstallation).

In PHP

The service Assets offers the same as methods, for a pattern written in PHP, a REST response or a mail. Inject it like any service:

use Saij\WpAppFramework\Asset\Assets;

final readonly class Newsletter
{
    public function __construct(private Assets $assets) {}

    public function header(): string
    {
        return $this->assets->image('images/logo.png', 'My Shop');
    }

    public function attachment(): string
    {
        return $this->assets->path('docs/terms.pdf');
    }
}

url(), image() and svg() work like the Twig functions. path() returns the file's path in the file system, for example to attach it to a mail. image() with variants needs the same variants as a call in a template: the build only reads the templates, not PHP code.

Overrides and the child theme's own files

A child theme replaces an asset of its parent theme with a file of the same name in overrides/{theme-slug}/assets/. The customer's logo needs no code: overrides/my-theme/assets/images/logo.svg in the child theme replaces assets/images/logo.svg of my-theme wherever the theme prints it. In the same way, a theme or child theme replaces an asset of a plugin in overrides/{plugin-slug}/assets/. The search order is the one of Twig templates, in the assets/ subdirectory of each override directory, so asset names never collide with template names.

A child theme can also bring files that replace nothing, for example in an override of a parent template. They live in the child theme's own assets/ directory, and their name starts with @child/:

{# overrides/my-theme/Block/Team/Team.twig in the child theme #}
{{ asset_image('@child/images/team.jpg', 'Our team') }}

@theme/… (in a plugin: @plugin/…) names the instance's own file and skips the overrides, like the prefixes of Twig templates.

No manifest knows the files of the overrides directories and of @child/, because they belong to another project. The framework therefore looks them up in the file system, only in override directories that exist, and versions their URL with their modification time.

Files the build references

A stylesheet or script can reference an asset itself: url('./fonts/inter.woff2') in assets/styles.css, or `` in a script. These references belong to the build of @wordpress/scripts, which copies such files with a hash in their name to build/images/ and build/fonts/ and writes their URL into the built file. They need nothing from the framework, and an override does not replace them.

Fonts are a common case. A plugin declares them with @font-face in its assets/styles.css. A theme can do the same, or declare them in theme.json, where WordPress resolves file:./assets/fonts/… relative to the theme. WordPress looks for such a file in the child theme first, so there a file of the same name in the child theme's assets/ replaces the theme's file without overrides/.

Rules

Function	Method of Assets	Returns
asset_url(name)	url(string $name): string	The URL with ?ver= and the hash of the content, or the modification time for files of overrides and @child/.
–	path(string $name): string	The path in the file system.
asset_image(name, alt, attributes = {})	image(string $name, string $alt, array $attributes = []): string	<img> with src, width and height (if known), alt, decoding="async" and the attributes; true adds an attribute without value, false leaves it out. src, srcset and alt cannot be set as attributes. With widths (list of pixel widths) and sizes the <img> gets a srcset; with formats (avif, webp, jpeg, png) a <picture> with a <source> per format around it.
asset_svg(name, attributes = {})	svg(string $name, array $attributes = []): string	The SVG markup without XML declaration and doctype, the attributes on its root element (class added, false removes).

A name is a path below assets/, without .. segments, without a leading / and without \. It may start with @child/ or with the instance's own prefix (@theme/ in themes and child themes, @plugin/ in plugins).

Static assets are all files in assets/ except hidden files (a name or directory starting with .) and .js, .jsx, .ts, .tsx, .css and .scss.

The build lists each asset with the first 12 characters of the xxh128 hash of its content, raster images with the width and height from their header, SVG images with width and height of the root element in pixels or without unit, otherwise with the size of its viewBox.

An unknown name, an invalid name, @child/ without an active child theme, asset_svg() with a file that is no .svg or has no root element <svg> are errors. In a block they behave like render errors: thrown with WP_DEBUG, otherwise logged, and the block renders nothing.

Overrides are searched in the order of the template lookup: for a theme the child theme's overrides/{theme-slug}/assets/, for a plugin those of the child theme and the theme. An override needs an asset of the same name in the instance.

The release ships assets/. A listed asset missing in the release – for example removed by export-ignore – stops the release build, and so does a missing image variant or a missing build/styles.preload.php for a stylesheet with marked fonts.

Image variants

The build reads every template in src/ with Twig's parser. A call of asset_image() whose literal attribute map has widths or formats asks for variants. A template that does not parse is skipped with a remark in the output of the build.

Build errors: a name that is no literal string, a name that is no static asset of the project or no JPEG, PNG, WebP or AVIF image, widths that are no literal list of positive integers (also missing with formats), widths without sizes, and formats that are no literal list of jpeg, png, webp and avif.

All calls of an image add up: the build creates every requested width in the format of the image and in every requested format. Widths larger than the image are left out.

A variant is {path without extension}.{hash}.{width}.{extension}, below generated/assets/ of a release and wp-content/cache/{slug}/assets/ in development mode, which also removes variants no template asks for any more. JPEG variants have quality 82, WebP 80, AVIF 60; metadata is removed.

At run time, a call that asks for variants the build did not create is an error. An image of an override or of the child theme is printed without variants.

Fonts and the media library

/* wp-app-framework: preload */ in an @font-face of assets/styles.css marks its first woff2 file of the project for preloading; a marked rule without one is a build error of the webpack helper. The preloads are printed on the front end only.

MediaLibrary::import(string $name): int imports an asset of the instance itself, also named with @theme/ or @plugin/; @child/ is an error. The attachment carries the meta key _wp_app_framework_asset with slug, name and hash of the asset.

Child themes are where customer projects adapt a theme: different markup for a block, adjusted colors, an extra block or two. The framework supports two kinds of child theme, and you pick the one that fits the job.

Child themes without code

Most adaptations need no PHP at all. A child theme without code overrides templates of its parent theme and adjusts theme.json, block templates, template parts and patterns, as WordPress allows for any child theme. It needs no Composer, no boot and no build. Without a build, nothing checks its block templates, template parts and patterns against the blocks of the parent theme (see Checking templates, parts and patterns).

To change the markup of a block, put a template with the same path into overrides/{parent-slug}/. For the parent theme my-theme, the file overrides/my-theme/Block/Hero/Hero.twig replaces the theme's src/Block/Hero/Hero.twig. The block keeps its attributes and its editor; only the markup changes. Twig templates describes the full lookup order.

Files work the same way: overrides/my-theme/assets/images/logo.svg replaces the theme's assets/images/logo.svg, and files that replace nothing go into the child theme's own assets/ and are named @child/… (see Static assets).

The overrides can use the classes in the parent theme's stylesheet, or plain CSS in the child theme's style.css. If they need further Tailwind utilities, the child theme needs code and a build of its own (see Styles).

Child themes with code

As soon as a child theme brings code of its own – blocks, hooks, REST routes – it becomes a framework instance of its own, just like a plugin. It has:

its own composer.json with type: wordpress-theme, while its style.css names the parent theme in Template:;

its own PSR-4 namespace and its own namespace for blocks, REST routes and settings (extra.wp-app-framework.namespace);

its own release, prefixed with its own namespace;

ChildTheme::boot() in its functions.php (see Boot and modes).

Why a separate instance instead of extending the parent's? WordPress loads the child theme's functions.php before the parent's, and in a release the parent's framework carries the parent's prefix. The child theme cannot rely on the parent's framework classes, so it brings its own. The two instances share nothing.

The build records the parent theme in the manifest, and the boot fails if another parent theme is active. Block names that collide with the parent's are errors, as between any two instances.

The src/ directory of a child theme with code belongs to its own instance. Its templates are searched there and in the framework, and they never override the theme's templates. Overrides of the parent still go into overrides/{parent-slug}/.

Using the parent theme

The child theme reaches its parent theme only through the parent's public interface (see Plugins).

Template callables. The child theme's templates call the parent's public template functions and filters directly. Unlike with plugins, the child theme doesn't need to list the parent in its composer.json.

Public API. The child theme receives the parent's public API through a constructor parameter with #[ThemeApi] (Saij\WpAppFramework\Api\ThemeApi):

use My\Theme\Api\SectionsApi;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Api\ThemeApi;

final class Teasers
{
    public function __construct(
        #[ThemeApi]
        private readonly ?SectionsApi $sections = null,
    ) {}
}

The parent theme boots after the child theme, so the service must not be created before after_setup_theme. Require the parent theme as a development dependency for your IDE and static analysis.

Child themes offer no public interface of their own.

Some tasks don't belong in a web request: importing a feed with thousands of entries, cleaning up old data, migrating content after a change of its structure. WP-CLI runs such tasks from the command line, without a time limit and without a browser.

A plain WP-CLI command is a class or closure that you register with WP_CLI::add_command(). It receives its arguments as an array of strings, and its help text and the rules for its arguments live in a doc comment that WP-CLI parses. WP-CLI only checks the form of the arguments: --limit=many reaches your code as the string "many".

With the framework, a command is a method with #[CliSubcommand]. The build derives the command line from the method signature, and the framework converts every value to the declared type before your method runs. Like a hook or a REST route, the class is a service: it gets its dependencies injected and is created only when the command runs.

Commands, groups and the namespace

Every command has three parts: wp {namespace} {command} {subcommand}. The namespace is the one of your theme or plugin (extra.wp-app-framework.namespace, see Concepts). The command is a group named by #[CliCommand] on a class, the subcommand a method of that class.

The namespace comes first, so commands of different themes and plugins never collide with each other. WP-CLI itself would not prevent that. A second command with an existing name is silently merged with the first one, and one of them loses its description or subcommands.

wp sections                         # lists the groups of the theme
wp sections events                  # lists the subcommands of the group
wp sections events import <url>     # runs a subcommand
wp help sections events import      # shows its help

A first command

Start with a class that imports events. #[CliCommand] gives the group its name and a one-line description:

use Saij\WpAppFramework\WpCli\CliCommand;

#[CliCommand('events', 'Manage the events of the theme.')]
final class EventCommands
{
    public function __construct(private readonly EventImporter $importer) {}
}

Each subcommand is a public method that returns void. #[CliSubcommand] names it and describes it in one line; longDescription adds text that the help shows after the options, typically examples:

use Saij\WpAppFramework\WpCli\Argument;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\WpCli\CliException;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\WpCli\CliOutput;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\WpCli\CliSubcommand;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\WpCli\Option;

#[CliSubcommand('import', 'Import events from a feed.', longDescription: "## EXAMPLES\n\n    wp sections events import https://example.com/feed --limit=10")]
public function import(
    CliOutput $output,
    #[Argument('URL of the feed.')]
    string $url,
    #[Option('Maximum number of events.')]
    int $limit = 50,
    #[Option('How existing events are treated.')]
    ImportMode $mode = ImportMode::Merge,
    #[Option('Only show what would be imported.')]
    bool $dryRun = false,
): void {
    $count = $this->importer->import($url, $limit, $mode, $dryRun);
    if ($count === 0) {
        throw new CliException('The feed contained no events.', 2);
    }
    $output->success(sprintf('%d events imported.', $count));
}

With the namespace sections, this is the command wp sections events import <url> [--limit=<limit>] [--mode=<mode>] [--dry-run]. Read the signature from left to right:

CliOutput $output receives the output of the command; the framework passes it in.

#[Argument] string $url is a positional argument, <url>. It has no default, so it is required.

#[Option] int $limit = 50 is an option, --limit=<limit>. Without it, the method receives the default 50; with --limit=many, the command stops with Error: Invalid value for --limit: limit is not of type integer. before your method runs.

#[Option] ImportMode $mode is a backed enum. The help lists its values, and only these are accepted; the method receives the enum case.

#[Option] bool $dryRun = false is a flag, --dry-run. WP-CLI also understands --no-dry-run.

A parameter typed with a service, such as a repository, is injected like in a hook. Each parameter must be one of these kinds; a parameter that is neither the output nor a service nor marked with #[Argument] or #[Option] is a build error. The marker is required so that a typo in a type can't turn a service into an argument without you noticing.

Arguments and options

Parameter names become kebab-case: $dryRun and $dry_run both become --dry-run, so they can't appear in the same method. The names can't be changed otherwise; rename the parameter instead.

A positional argument with a default value is optional, [<count>]. A variadic argument takes all remaining values and needs at least one:

#[CliSubcommand('delete', 'Delete events.')]
public function delete(CliOutput $output, #[Argument('IDs of the events.')] int ...$ids): void
{
    // wp sections events delete 12 13 14
}

An option without default value is required: WP-CLI stops with missing --mode parameter when it is left out. A nullable option or argument needs a default value, usually null; the method receives the default when the value is not given.

The values are checked and converted by the same rules as REST arguments: "5" becomes the integer 5, "0.5" the float 0.5, and a backed enum accepts exactly its values. WP-CLI itself would also accept a comma-separated list of enum values such as --mode=merge,replace; the framework rejects it, because the parameter takes one value.

Output and errors

Write output through CliOutput, never with echo, so that WP-CLI can control it:

Method	Output
log(string $message)	the message on stdout
success(string $message)	Success: {message} on stdout
warning(string $message)	Warning: {message} on stderr
table(array $rows, array $fields)	the rows as a table with the given columns

--quiet suppresses the messages. CliOutput is passed to the method; it is no service, so a constructor can't ask for it.

To end a command with an error, throw a CliException. WP-CLI prints Error: {message} and exits with the exit code of the exception, 1 by default. Scripts that call your command can tell different failures apart by the exit code; it must be between 1 and 255.

Any other exception is unexpected. The framework writes it to the PHP error log with the command and the method. With WP_DEBUG enabled, it rethrows the exception, so PHP reports it with its stack trace; WordPress then ends the command with its message about a critical error. Without WP_DEBUG, the command ends with Error: Command failed: {exception class}: {message}. In both cases the exit code is 1, unless the site disables the fatal error handler of WordPress.

Development mode writes a cache. The framework builds the manifest on the first run and caches it in wp-content/cache/{slug}/. Run wp as the user of the web server (e.g. docker compose exec -u www-data …), not with --allow-root: files that root creates there can't be updated by the web server afterwards.

Commands come with their theme or plugin. WP-CLI loads WordPress with the active theme and plugins, which register their commands. With --skip-themes or --skip-plugins, the commands of the skipped consumers don't exist.

The namespace must be free. If another plugin or package already registers a command with the name of your namespace, the framework doesn't register your commands and prints a warning instead. The build rejects a namespace that is a command of WP-CLI itself, such as post or cache.

Rules

Attribute	Arguments	Target
#[CliCommand(name, description)]	name of the group, one-line description	a service class
#[CliSubcommand(name, description, longDescription: '')]	name, one-line description, text after the options in the help; repeatable	a public method of that class
#[Argument(description: '')]	description in the help	a parameter: positional argument <name>
#[Option(description: '')]	description in the help	a parameter: option --name=<name>, or a flag --name for bool

Type	As argument	As option
string, int, float, backed enum	yes, also variadic	yes
nullable (?int …)	with a default value	with a default value
bool	no	a flag, declared as bool $name = false
array, value classes, references, union types, mixed	no	no

The build reports, with class, method and parameter:

#[CliSubcommand] methods in a class without #[CliCommand], or #[CliCommand] without #[CliSubcommand] methods;

a class that is no service candidate; a method that is not public, is static or doesn't return void;

a command or subcommand name that doesn't start with a lowercase letter followed by lowercase letters, digits and -; a group name used twice in the project, a subcommand name used twice in a group;

a description that is empty, has a line break or more than 80 characters;

a parameter that is neither CliOutput, nor a service, nor marked; a parameter with both markers; a marker on the output or a service;

a type from the table that is not supported; #[Param], #[PostReference], #[TermReference] or #[UserReference]; a by-reference parameter; a variadic option;

a nullable argument or option without default value; a flag that is not bool $name = false; a variadic argument after an optional one;

two parameters with the same name in kebab-case; an option named like a global parameter of WP-CLI (--url, --path, --user, --debug, --quiet …) or --help; an option starting with no-, which WP-CLI reads as negated flag;

a project namespace that is a command of WP-CLI, such as post, cache or option, as soon as the project has commands.

Many blocks hold other blocks: a section holds teasers, tabs hold one panel per tab, a slider holds slides. In WordPress these are blocks with inner blocks. A container decides which blocks may go inside, which ones a new container starts with, and how editors may change them. The blocks inside often depend on their container, too: a tab only makes sense inside tabs, and it needs to know which tab is active.

This page shows how to build such a pair of blocks. The basics of a block – attribute class, template, render() – are explained in Blocks.

A container and its children

A block becomes a container when its render() accepts string $content, the rendered inner blocks. Its template marks the element that holds them with inner_blocks():

#[Block('tabs', title: 'Tabs', category: BlockCategory::Design)]
final class Tabs
{
    public function __construct(private TemplateRenderer $twig) {}

    public function render(TabsAttributes $attributes, string $content): string
    {
        return $this->twig->render('Block/Tabs/Tabs.twig', ['attrs' => $attributes, 'content' => $content]);
    }
}

<div {{ block_wrapper_attributes() }}>
  <div{{ inner_blocks() }}>{{ content|raw }}</div>
</div>

Without further declarations every block may be inserted into it, and the editor behaves as for core/group. #[InnerBlocks] changes that.

Inner blocks

#[InnerBlocks] on the block class describes the inner blocks:

#[Block('tabs', title: 'Tabs', category: BlockCategory::Design)]
#[InnerBlocks(
    allowed: [Tab::class],
    template: [new TemplateBlock(Tab::class, ['title' => 'Tab 1']), new TemplateBlock(Tab::class, ['title' => 'Tab 2'])],
    templateLock: TemplateLock::Insert,
    orientation: Orientation::Horizontal,
)]
final class Tabs { /* … */ }

allowed limits the blocks that can be inserted. Here only tabs.

template is what a newly inserted container starts with: two tabs with their titles.

templateLock restricts changes. TemplateLock::Insert lets editors reorder the tabs, but not add or remove them; TemplateLock::All allows no changes to the structure, TemplateLock::ContentOnly only changes of the content. Without it the container inherits the lock of its parent, and TemplateLock::Unlocked lifts a lock of the parent.

orientation tells the editor that the tabs sit side by side. The movers then show arrows to the left and right.

Three more options control how blocks are added. defaultBlock is the block the editor suggests, and with directInsert: true the "+" at the end of the container inserts it right away. prioritized lists blocks that the quick inserter of the container – the small "+" – and the slash command offer first. appender chooses that "+" at the end: Appender::Default lets WordPress decide (an empty paragraph to type into, or the "+" button if paragraphs are not allowed), Appender::Button always shows the button, and Appender::None shows nothing, so blocks are added through the toolbar or the list view only.

With TemplateLock::All or TemplateLock::ContentOnly, the editor brings the inner blocks of every container in line with the template whenever it opens one. Inner blocks that differ – in a post, a template or a pattern – are replaced. The build reports templates, template parts and patterns whose inner blocks differ from a locked template.

Referring to blocks

allowed and the other options name blocks in three ways, so that your IDE can resolve them wherever possible:

blocks of your theme or plugin by their class, Tab::class;

core blocks by a case of CoreBlock, CoreBlock::Paragraph;

other blocks by their full name, 'acme/slider'.

A TemplateBlock takes the block in the same way, its attribute values and, for a container, its inner blocks: new TemplateBlock(Tab::class, ['title' => 'Tab 1'], [new TemplateBlock(CoreBlock::Paragraph)]). Attribute values of your own blocks are checked in the build against their attribute class; a backed enum stands for its value.

Restricting where a block goes

A tab outside of tabs makes no sense. parent on the child limits it to the containers it may be inserted into directly; the inserter then offers it only there:

#[Block('tab', title: 'Tab', category: BlockCategory::Design, parent: [Tabs::class])]
final class Tab { /* … */ }

ancestor is the looser variant: the block may be inserted anywhere inside the named blocks, also deeper down. Both take the same values as allowed. The build checks that each container of your own in parent allows the child, and it reports your blocks placed elsewhere in templates, template parts and patterns.

Passing values to inner blocks

The tabs know which tab is active; each tab needs that value to render itself open or closed. WordPress passes such values as block context: a container provides values of its attributes, and the blocks inside read them, at any depth, without storing them themselves.

The container marks the attributes it passes on with #[ProvidesContext] in its attribute class:

final readonly class TabsAttributes
{
    public function __construct(
        #[ProvidesContext]
        public int $active = 0,
        public TabStyle $style = TabStyle::Line,
    ) {}
}

A block inside reads the value with a parameter of render() marked #[Context]. It names the container class and its attribute:

final class Tab
{
    public function render(
        TabAttributes $attributes,
        string $content,
        #[Context(Tabs::class, 'active')]
        int $active = 0,
    ): string {
        // …
    }
}

The parameter receives the value with its type, created like an attribute: an enum as its case, a value class as an object. Outside of tabs there is no value, and the parameter keeps its default; that's why it needs a default or a nullable type. In the editor the value reaches the block as well: when an editor changes the active tab, the tabs inside render again.

The attribute name is the only string here, and the build checks it: the container must be a block of your theme or plugin, its attribute class must have the attribute, the attribute must be marked with #[ProvidesContext], and the parameter must have the attribute's type.

WordPress itself names context values; the framework derives the names, {namespace}/{block}/{attribute} – here sections/tabs/active. Values of other blocks, such as postId in a query loop, are read as before with usesContext and WP_Block (see Blocks).

Rules

#[InnerBlocks(allowed: null, template: null, templateLock: null, orientation: null, defaultBlock: null, directInsert: false, prioritized: [], appender: Appender::Default)]:

Argument	Type	WordPress
allowed	list of block references, null for all	allowed_blocks of the block type
template	list of TemplateBlock	template of useInnerBlocksProps()
templateLock	TemplateLock (All, Insert, ContentOnly, Unlocked = false), null to inherit	templateLock
orientation	Orientation (Horizontal, Vertical)	orientation
defaultBlock	TemplateBlock without inner blocks	defaultBlock
directInsert	bool; needs defaultBlock	directInsert
prioritized	list of block references, each in allowed	prioritizedInserterBlocks
appender	Appender (Default, Button, None)	renderAppender

A block reference is a block class of the same theme or plugin, a CoreBlock case or the full name of another block. The build reports:

#[InnerBlocks] on a block whose render() takes no string $content;

a class that is no block of the theme or plugin, a name without /, a name in your own namespace (name those blocks by class) and a core block named as string although CoreBlock has a case for it – strings are only for core blocks newer than the framework;

template blocks, a default block or prioritized blocks that the container does not allow, inner blocks in a template block of a block without inner blocks, a default block with inner blocks, and directInsert without defaultBlock;

unknown or invalid attribute values of your own blocks in a TemplateBlock;

parent naming a container of your own that does not allow the block.

#[Block(parent: null, ancestor: null)] takes lists of block references.

Context:

#[ProvidesContext] marks parameters of a block attribute class only, not of value classes or settings groups. The value is passed as context {namespace}/{block}/{attribute}.

#[Context(Block::class, 'attribute')] marks parameters of render() only. The block must be a block of the same theme or plugin, the attribute marked with #[ProvidesContext], the parameter type that of the attribute (it may be nullable where the attribute is not), and the parameter needs a default or a nullable type.

usesContext must not name context of your own blocks; use #[Context] instead.

Some blocks need JavaScript on the page: an FAQ that opens its answers, a slider, a counter. The framework gives every such script the same shape. It is a class next to the block, which the framework loads only on pages that show the block, starts once per block and stops again when needed. In the editor, content editors can switch a block to a preview and try its behavior right there.

Other scripts belong to no block: a mobile menu, a cookie notice, smooth scrolling. They run on every page as document views, in the same shape.

This page explains how a front-end script – a view script – is written, when it runs and how the preview in the editor works. It then covers scripts for the whole page, data from PHP, external scripts and modules shared between scripts.

How it works

A view script lives next to its block class, like an editor script: src/Block/Faq/Faq.view.js next to src/Block/Faq/Faq.php. Your build compiles it with the webpack helper, together with the framework's view runtime, a small script that provides the base classes and starts the views.

When WordPress renders a block that has a view script, the framework does two things. It adds a marker attribute to the block's root element, through block_wrapper_attributes(). And it enqueues the view runtime and the block's script for this page. A page without the block loads neither of them.

Once the page has been parsed, the view runtime finds the marked root elements and creates one instance of the script's class for each, then calls its mount() method. Two FAQ blocks on a page are two independent instances, each working only inside its own block.

A block read from the cache enqueues its view scripts as well. The scripts load deferred, at the end of the page, so they never block rendering. They are ordinary scripts and may import WordPress' packages such as @wordpress/dom-ready or @wordpress/api-fetch.

An FAQ, step by step

The block renders a question as a button and the answer below it. The answer starts hidden on the front end. In the editor it stays visible, so it can be edited inline:

{# src/Block/Faq/Faq.twig #}
<div {{ block_wrapper_attributes({class: 'faq'}) }}>
    <button type="button" class="faq__question" aria-expanded="false"{{ field('question') }}>{{ attrs.question }}</button>
    <div class="faq__answer"{{ field('answer') }}{% if not is_editor() %} hidden{% endif %}>{{ attrs.answer|raw }}</div>
</div>

The root element must be printed with block_wrapper_attributes(): that is where the marker goes. Without it, rendering the block fails with an error.

Next to the block class goes the view script. It exports a class that extends BlockView:

// src/Block/Faq/Faq.view.js


export default class Faq extends BlockView {
	mount() {
		this.question = this.find( '.faq__question' );
		this.answer = this.find( '.faq__answer' );
		this.listen( this.question, 'click', () => this.toggle() );
	}

	toggle() {
		const open = this.answer.hidden;
		this.answer.hidden = ! open;
		this.question.setAttribute( 'aria-expanded', String( open ) );
	}
}

mount() sets the view up. find() looks for an element inside the block, so a second FAQ on the page is never touched. listen() adds an event listener the framework removes again when the view stops. You never call document.querySelectorAll() yourself and never clean up listeners by hand.

That is all. Run npm run build, add the block to a page, and the answer opens and closes.

Life cycle

The view runtime creates the instance and calls mount(). On the front end a view lives as long as the page. In the editor preview, it stops when the preview ends, when the block renders again after a change, and when the block is removed. Stopping calls unmount() and then undoes everything registered with listen() and onUnmount(), last first.

Most views need only mount(). For resources listen() does not cover, such as an observer or a timer, register the cleanup right where you create the resource:

mount() {
	const observer = new this.window.IntersectionObserver( ( entries ) => { … } );
	observer.observe( this.element );
	this.onUnmount( () => observer.disconnect() );
}

Override unmount() only for work that must happen before those cleanups. Do not override the constructor: it belongs to the framework, and a view that overrides it fails to mount.

Block supports

A block support can have a view script too, next to its class: src/Support/Reveal.view.js. It runs in every block that uses the support, as a separate instance on the same root element as the block's own view. Its class extends SupportView, which adds this.value, the support's value in this block:

// src/Support/Reveal.view.js


export default class Reveal extends SupportView {
	mount() {
		if ( this.value ) {
			this.element.dataset.revealed = 'true';
			this.onUnmount( () => delete this.element.dataset.revealed );
		}
	}
}

A page loads a support's script once, however many blocks use it. A block that uses a support with a view script needs block_wrapper_attributes() like a block with its own view script.

Scripts for the whole page

A script that belongs to no block is a document view. It lives in the assets/ directory of the theme or plugin (see Concepts), with a name ending in .view.js: assets/menu.view.js, or deeper, assets/nav/sticky.view.js. It needs no PHP class. The framework loads it on every page of the front end, deferred in the footer like the other view scripts, and never in the editor.

Its class extends DocumentView. The view runtime creates one instance per page, for the document element, and mounts it before the views of the blocks:

// assets/menu.view.js


export default class Menu extends DocumentView {
	mount() {
		const toggle = this.find( '.menu-toggle' );
		if ( ! toggle ) {
			return;
		}
		this.listen( toggle, 'click', () =>
			this.element.classList.toggle( 'menu-open' )
		);
	}
}

find() and findAll() search the whole document here. A document view has no condition in PHP: when a page does not need it, the script decides that itself and returns early, as above. Behavior that belongs to one block of your own is a view script of that block instead, which loads only with the block.

Data from PHP

Scripts often need values that only the server knows: a setting, a key for a map service, a URL. Set them on the service ViewData, which you inject anywhere – in a hook, in a block's render():

use Saij\WpAppFramework\Hook\Hook;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\View\ViewData;

final readonly class MapData
{
    public function __construct(private MapSettings $settings) {}

    #[Hook('wp')]
    public function provide(ViewData $viewData): void
    {
        $viewData->set('map', ['zoom' => $this->settings->zoom]);
    }
}

Every view reads a value with this.data( key ). Editor scripts and shared modules import getData instead:

mount() {
	const { zoom } = this.data( 'map' );
}

The values belong to the instance: all views and editor scripts of the theme or plugin see them, other instances do not. On the front end the framework prints them with the view runtime in the footer, so values set while blocks render are included. A cached block stores the values its render() set and sets them again when it comes from the cache. In the editor the framework prints the values at the end of enqueue_block_editor_assets, so set them on that action or earlier.

Every visitor can read the values in the page source; never pass secrets. Values that differ per user, such as a nonce, do not suit pages a page cache stores for everyone. Values about a single block belong into its markup, as data- attributes the block's view reads.

External scripts

Some libraries come only from their provider's server, such as a payment form or a map service. A view loads such a script itself, when it mounts:

async mount() {
	await this.loadScript( 'https://js.example.com/v3/', { crossorigin: 'anonymous' } );
	this.map = new this.window.ExampleMaps.Map( this.find( '.map' ) );
}

The script loads only on pages where the view mounts, so a block's library loads only with the block. Each URL loads once per document, also when several views or several themes and plugins ask for it; the promise of every call settles when the script has loaded. Outside a view, import loadScript from @saij/wp-app-framework/view, or from @saij/wp-app-framework in editor scripts.

The download starts only when the view mounts. Whether a visitor has agreed to a third-party script is up to your code.

Shared modules

Each view script is built as a bundle of its own. A module that two scripts import is therefore two copies, each with its own state. A module that several scripts share – a dialog manager, a store – goes into assets/shared/ instead: assets/shared/dialogs.js. Scripts import it as #shared/dialogs:

// assets/shared/dialogs.js
const open = new Set();
export function register( dialog ) {
	open.add( dialog );
}

// src/Block/Modal/Modal.view.js

The build turns every module in assets/shared/ into a script of its own. A script that imports it depends on it, so WordPress loads the module once, before the scripts that use it, and all of them share the same instance. View scripts, document views, editor scripts and other shared modules can import shared modules.

The prefix # makes the import a subpath import of Node. Map it in the package.json of the theme or plugin, so that your IDE finds the files; the build fails without it:

{
	"imports": { "#shared/*": "./assets/shared/*.js" }
}

Preview in the editor

In the editor, a block shows its editing view: fields are edited inline, and empty fields stay visible. View scripts do not run there, because clicks belong to editing. A block with a view script – its own or one of its supports – gets a Preview button in its toolbar instead.

The preview shows the block as it appears on the front end. The editor asks the server to render the block, with its inner blocks, exactly as on a page, and shows that markup in place of the editing view. Then it mounts the block's views on it, with this.isPreview set to true. Content editors can open the FAQ, step through a slider or try any other state.

Editing pauses while the preview is on; the inspector stays available. A changed attribute renders the preview again: the old views unmount, the new markup appears and new views mount. Pressing Preview again, or removing the block, unmounts the views and returns to editing. Buttons of your own in a block's toolbar are an editor feature, independent of view scripts; see Toolbar buttons.

Two things differ from the front end, and the view must allow for them:

Another document. The editor runs view scripts in its main window, but the block lives in the canvas iframe. Use this.document and this.window, which belong to the block's element, instead of the globals document and window.

The root element belongs to the editor. In the preview, the editor manages the class and style of the root element and resets them, for example when the block is selected. Keep the state of a view in the elements inside the block or in data attributes, not in the root element's classes.

Rules

Files

Script	Location	Default export
view script of a block	next to the block class: Faq.php → Faq.view.js	a class extending BlockView
view script of a block support	next to the support class: Reveal.php → Reveal.view.js	a class extending SupportView
document view	in assets/: assets/menu.view.js	a class extending DocumentView
shared module	in assets/shared/: assets/shared/dialogs.js, imported as #shared/dialogs	any exports

A view script outside the directory your build compiles (src/) is a build error, and so is one in src/ without a block or block support class next to it. A view script that imports @saij/wp-app-framework – the editor runtime, which a page does not load – fails the build of the webpack helper, also when it imports it through shared modules; view scripts import @saij/wp-app-framework/view. An import of a shared module that does not exist, shared modules that import each other in a cycle, and shared modules without the imports entry in package.json fail the build as well. Two scripts in assets/ whose names give the same script handle are a build error.

BlockView, SupportView and DocumentView

Member	Meaning
mount()	Override: set the view up.
unmount()	Override if needed: runs before the cleanups of listen() and onUnmount().
element	The root element of the block.
document, window	The document and window of the element; in the editor preview those of the canvas iframe.
isPreview	true in the editor preview.
find( selector )	The first matching element in the block, or null.
findAll( selector )	All matching elements in the block, in document order.
listen( target, type, handler, options )	Adds an event listener, removed when the view unmounts.
onUnmount( callback )	Runs a function when the view unmounts.
data( key )	A value set with ViewData::set(); an unknown key throws an error that names the known ones.
loadScript( url, attributes )	Loads an external script into the view's document; see below.
value	SupportView only: the value of the support in this block.

In a block's view, find() and findAll() skip the elements of nested blocks that have view scripts of their own; those blocks get their own views. In a DocumentView, element is the document element, find() and findAll() search the whole document and isPreview is always false.

ViewData

Method	Meaning
set( string $key, mixed $value )	Sets a value; a later call with the same key replaces it. The key consists of letters, digits, - and _ and starts with a letter. The value is null, a scalar or an array of these; objects and the floats NAN and INF throw.
has( string $key ), get( string $key )	Read a value in PHP; get() throws for an unknown key.

A value set after the values were printed – after the footer scripts of the front end, after enqueue_block_editor_assets in the editor – throws with WP_DEBUG and is logged otherwise. The values are printed only where a runtime of the instance loads: on the front end with the view runtime, in the editor with the editor runtime. In JavaScript, getData( key ) of @saij/wp-app-framework/view and @saij/wp-app-framework reads them like this.data( key ).

loadScript( url, attributes )

url is an https: URL or a protocol-relative one (//…); any other URL rejects the promise.

attributes are further attributes of the script element, such as integrity, crossorigin or data-…; true adds an attribute without value, false leaves it out.

The promise is fulfilled when the script has loaded and rejected when it fails to load, also for later calls with the same URL.

A URL loads once per document. A later call with other attributes warns on the console and keeps the first ones. A script element with the URL that the page already has, for example one WordPress printed, counts as loaded.

Loading

A block with a view script, or with a block support that has one, must print its root element with block_wrapper_attributes(); otherwise rendering it fails with an error.

The view runtime ({slug}-wp-app-framework-view) and the scripts ({slug}-{namespace}-{name}-view, {slug}-support-{attribute}-view) are enqueued while the block renders, deferred in the footer. They are not enqueued for automatic excerpts, and WordPress removes them again if the block renders nothing.

Document views load on every page of the front end (wp_enqueue_scripts) with the view runtime, deferred in the footer, with the handle {slug}-document-{path}-view (assets/nav/sticky.view.js → my-theme-document-nav-sticky-view). They do not load in the admin, in the editor or in its preview.

The views mount on DOMContentLoaded: first the document views in the order of their paths, then one instance per script and root element of the blocks – the block's view first, then the views of its supports in the order of #[UseSupport]. A script that is not loaded, or whose default export does not extend the right base class, is reported on the console; the other views still mount.

A shared module has the handle {slug}-shared-{name} (#shared/ui/Dialog → my-theme-shared-ui-dialog) and is registered when a script that depends on it is registered; one that no script on the page uses does not load.

In the editor, the view runtime and the view scripts of all blocks load with the editor runtime. A block with a view script gets the Preview toolbar button. The preview renders the block with its inner blocks through POST /wp-app-framework/v1/{slug}/preview (edit_posts, and edit_post for the post), as on the front end.

A plugin uses the framework just like a theme. It boots its own instance with Plugin::boot(__FILE__) (see Boot and modes) and has its own blocks, hooks, REST routes, templates and settings. Everything in the guides applies to plugins as well.

What is special about plugins is that they often work together with a theme. A plugin may provide data the theme displays, or helpers the theme's templates call. This page explains how a plugin – or a theme, towards its child theme – offers such an interface, and how the theme changes the plugin's markup.

Why a public interface

In a release, every instance ships its own copy of the framework and its dependencies, prefixed with its own namespace. The theme's Twig\Markup and the plugin's Twig\Markup are different classes with different names. Instances therefore never share PHP objects of the framework or its dependencies, and passing one across would fail.

The interface between instances is built from two things that every instance understands:

WordPress hooks named after the provider's slug. The framework uses them under the hood to hand over services and template callables.

Signatures with plain types. Methods offered to others may use only PHP types, WordPress types and the provider's own classes. The provider's build rejects anything else, including in the project classes such a signature uses.

The provider is responsible for keeping its interface compatible across its own versions.

Public API

#[PublicApi] (Saij\WpAppFramework\Api\PublicApi) marks a service of a plugin, or of a theme, as its API:

namespace My\Plugin\Api;

use Saij\WpAppFramework\Api\PublicApi;

#[PublicApi]
final class NoteApi
{
    public function countNotes(int $postId): int { /* … */ }
}

Another consumer receives the API of a plugin through a constructor parameter with #[PluginApi]:

use My\Plugin\Api\NoteApi;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Api\PluginApi;

final class Notes
{
    public function __construct(
        #[PluginApi('my-plugin')]
        private readonly ?NoteApi $notes = null,
    ) {}
}

The plugin may be deactivated, so the parameter must be nullable with the default null. (What happens when a plugin is activated, deactivated and deleted is described in Activation, deactivation and uninstallation.) It is null whenever the plugin is not active, and your code checks for that.

The consumer's build does not load the class, so it works without the plugin. For your IDE and static analysis, require the plugin as a development dependency (require-dev); the release build installs without development dependencies.

The API service is created together with the consumer's service. Creating it before plugins_loaded is an error, because plugins may not be loaded yet. An active plugin that does not provide the requested class is an error as well.

Public template functions and filters

A plugin can also offer Twig functions and filters to the templates of other consumers. #[TemplateFunction(name, public: true)] and #[TemplateFilter(name, public: true)] mark them. Themes can do the same for their child themes.

The name must start with the provider's template prefix: its slug with underscores, for example my_plugin_ for my-plugin. That keeps public names from colliding with the consumer's own.

A consumer lists the plugins whose callables it uses in its composer.json:

"extra": { "wp-app-framework": { "namespace": "my-theme", "template-plugins": ["my-plugin"] } }

Its templates then call them by name:

{% if has_plugin('my-plugin') %}
    {{ my_plugin_note_count() }} {{ title|my_plugin_highlight }}
{% endif %}

A child theme uses the callables of its parent theme without listing it.

Twig resolves names while it compiles a template, which may happen while the plugin is inactive. A name with a declared prefix therefore compiles without the plugin and is resolved only when it is called. Calling it without the plugin is an error, so check with has_plugin(slug) first.

A few more rules:

Arguments are positional.

Results of html: true callables are printed unescaped.

The input of such an HTML filter is escaped first, unless it is the result of an HTML function or filter. Unlike filters of your own instance, a value marked with |raw is escaped as well.

Names starting with a declared prefix are reserved; the consumer's own callables must not use them.

Template overrides

A theme changes the markup of a plugin's blocks by overriding its templates in overrides/{plugin-slug}/. For example, overrides/my-plugin/Block/Note/Note.twig in the theme replaces src/Block/Note/Note.twig of the plugin. Twig templates describes the full lookup order. A file of the plugin's assets/ directory is replaced the same way, in overrides/{plugin-slug}/assets/ (see Static assets).

Styles

Class names are global, so the stylesheet of a plugin uses a Tailwind prefix. Its classes never meet those of the theme (see Styles).

Many sites need content of their own kind besides posts and pages: events, team members, venues, products. WordPress calls these post types, and their extra values post meta. Registering them by hand means a long list of arguments for register_post_type(), a meta key per value with a schema for the REST API, a form in the editor and code that reads the stored strings back into typed values.

The framework lets you declare all of this with classes, as you do with blocks and settings:

a post class declares a post type and is at the same time the object of a post of that type: you load, change and save posts through it;

a meta class declares typed fields that belong to posts of one or more post types;

a taxonomy class declares a taxonomy for post types.

The build checks the declarations, and the framework registers everything on init, adds the forms to the editor and keeps the URLs working.

A post type

A post class extends AbstractPost and carries #[PostType]:

use Saij\WpAppFramework\Post\AbstractPost;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Post\PostType;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Post\PostTypeSupport;

#[PostType('event', label: 'Events', singularLabel: 'Event', supports: [PostTypeSupport::Title, PostTypeSupport::Editor], archive: true)]
final class Event extends AbstractPost {}

The framework registers the post type under the namespace of your theme or plugin: with the namespace sections, event becomes sections_event. That is the name WordPress stores with every post, so it must not change later. The post type is always shown in the REST API, which the block editor needs.

WordPress would label the new type with the texts of posts ("Add New Post"). The framework derives all labels from label and singularLabel instead: "Add New Event", "Edit Event", "No events found." and so on. The labels are English; when you need other texts, set them in labels:

#[PostType('event', label: 'Veranstaltungen', singularLabel: 'Veranstaltung', labels: new PostTypeLabels(addNewItem: 'Neue Veranstaltung', editItem: 'Veranstaltung bearbeiten'))]

The URL of an event uses the name without namespace, with - instead of _: /event/summer-concert/. With archive: true the type also has an archive at /event/. slug sets another one. Block themes show posts of the type with the templates single-sections_event.html and archive-sections_event.html, which fall back to single.html and archive.html.

Data types

Not every post type is a page of its own. A venue that events refer to is data: it needs no URL and no block content.

#[PostType('venue', label: 'Venues', singularLabel: 'Venue', public: false, supports: [PostTypeSupport::Title])]
final class Venue extends AbstractPost {}

public: false hides it from the front end and searches; showUi (true by default) keeps it in the admin menu. Without PostTypeSupport::Editor, WordPress edits it on the classic edit screen instead of the block editor. The framework shows the meta of the type there as a form (see Editing).

Meta fields

Meta fields are declared in a class of their own, a meta class. Its promoted constructor parameters are the fields, usually private with getters and setters of your own:

use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\Label;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\RichText;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Post\PostMeta;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Value\ListOf;

#[PostMeta('details', title: 'Event details', postTypes: [Event::class])]
final class EventDetails
{
    /**
     * @param list<Ticket> $tickets
     */
    public function __construct(
        #[Label('Start')]
        private string $start = '',
        private int $capacity = 0,
        private ?Venue $venue = null,
        #[ListOf(Ticket::class)]
        private array $tickets = [],
        #[RichText(allowedFormats: ['core/bold', 'core/link'])]
        private string $note = '',
    ) {}

    public function getCapacity(): int
    {
        return $this->capacity;
    }

    public function setCapacity(int $capacity): void
    {
        $this->capacity = $capacity;
    }

    // …
}

Each field is stored in its own meta key: the namespace, _ and the field name in snake case, here sections_start, sections_capacity, sections_venue. The framework registers each key with register_post_meta(), with the schema of the field, so the REST API validates every value. Meta classes follow the rules of values with the markers of settings: rich text is edited with the classic editor, images with the media library.

A field typed as a post class – ?Venue here – refers to a post: it is stored as the ID and you get the object of the post. A list of #[ListOf(Venue::class)] stores IDs as well.

A Link – private Link $registration = new Link() – is stored as an object with the URL, the ID of the linked post and "Open in new tab", and you get it with the current URL of the post. #[PlainText(multiline: true)] keeps line breaks; print it with |nl2br.

One post type can have several meta classes, and one meta class can belong to several post types. Meta for the post types of WordPress names the framework classes Post and Page:

use Saij\WpAppFramework\Post\Page;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Post\Post;

#[PostMeta('extras', title: 'Extras', postTypes: [Post::class, Page::class])]
final class Extras
{
    public function __construct(
        #[PlainText]
        private string $subtitle = '',
    ) {}
}

Taxonomies

A taxonomy class carries #[Taxonomy] and has no fields:

#[Taxonomy('genre', label: 'Genres', singularLabel: 'Genre', postTypes: [Event::class, Post::class], hierarchical: true)]
final class Genre {}

It is registered as sections_genre, with labels derived like those of a post type. The block editor shows it in the document settings of its post types.

WordPress' own taxonomies have framework classes too: Category for category and Tag for post_tag. They register nothing; use them where a taxonomy class is expected, for example $post->getTerms(Category::class) or Terms::in(Tag::class, [$id]) in a query.

Working with posts

The post class loads posts, and meta() returns the meta objects of a post:

$event = Event::get($id);
$details = $event->meta(EventDetails::class);

$details->setCapacity(120);
$event->setTitle('Summer concert');
$event->save();

get() throws an exception if there is no event with the ID; find() returns null instead. meta() loads a meta object on first use and returns the same object afterwards, so changes stay until save().

save() checks every loaded meta object against its schema first. If a value is invalid, it throws a PostValidationException and writes nothing. Otherwise it saves the post and writes the meta values that changed since they were loaded; values that another request changed in the meantime stay as they are. A post created with new is inserted as a draft:

$event = new Event();
$event->setTitle('Autumn concert');
$event->meta(EventDetails::class)->setVenue(Venue::get($venueId));
$event->setTerms(Genre::class, [$jazzId]);
$event->save();

The base class provides the data of the post: getId(), title, content, excerpt, status (PostStatus), slug, dates, author, parent (hierarchical types), getUrl(), terms and toWpPost(). Your post class may add methods of its own, for example getCapacity() that reads the meta. It keeps no data of its own: a property in a post class is a build error, because it would not be stored.

Post, meta and taxonomy classes are data, not services: they cannot be injected. get() and find() work once the theme or plugin has booted.

To load a list of posts – filtered by meta fields or terms, sorted, in pages – start a query with Event::query(); Queries explains it.

Invalid stored values

Other code can store anything in a meta key. When a meta object is loaded, each stored value is checked against its schema; an invalid value is logged (wp-app-framework: post meta: …), and the field keeps the default of its constructor parameter. A reference to a post that no longer exists, has another type or is not published becomes null or is left out of its list.

Editing

In the block editor every meta class of the edited post type is a panel in the document settings, titled with the title of #[PostMeta]. The editor saves the fields together with the post, in the same request, and shows the error of an invalid value as a notice. Links are edited with the link control of the block editor.

On the classic edit screen – post types without the block editor – every meta class is a meta box with the same form; links have the form of the settings page, which searches posts only. Invalid values are not saved and listed in a notice after saving.

The framework removes WordPress' "Custom Fields" box from post types with meta: it would edit the meta keys as plain strings, past the schemas. Taxonomies are edited with the controls of WordPress.

Templates

post(id) returns the post object in Twig, for a post type of your theme or plugin, post or page. Getters are attributes, and meta classes are named by their #[PostMeta] name:

{% set event = post(postId) %}
{% if event %}
    <h2>{{ event.title }}</h2>
    <p>{{ event.meta('details').capacity }} seats at {{ event.meta('details').venue.title }}</p>
{% endif %}

Outside renders for the block editor, post() returns null for posts that are not published. Like everything in Twig, the values are escaped; print rich text with |raw.

Scalar meta fields also work with the post meta source of block bindings in templates, because their keys are shown in the REST API and not protected. A paragraph bound to sections_start shows the start of the event.

Rewrite rules

WordPress builds the URLs of post types and taxonomies only when its rewrite rules are flushed; usually you would visit the permalink settings after adding a post type. The framework does it for you: it keeps a hash of the declarations that affect URLs and flushes the rules when it changes. It also flushes after the theme was switched to and after the plugin was activated (see Activation, deactivation and uninstallation), since another theme or plugin may have flushed the rules without your post types in between. When a plugin is deleted, the framework deletes the posts of its post types, the terms of its taxonomies and its meta keys. The check costs no database query: the hash is stored in an autoloaded option, and a theme or plugin without post types and taxonomies only looks whether such an option is left over from earlier declarations. After a theme switch or deactivation, the old rules stay until the next flush; their URLs answer with "not found".

Rules

#[PostType(name, label, singularLabel, labels: null, description: '', public: true, showUi: true, supports: [Title, Editor], hierarchical: false, archive: false, slug: null, menuIcon: null, menuPosition: null)] on a subclass of AbstractPost:

name: lowercase letters, digits and _, starting with a letter; the full name {namespace}_{name} has at most 20 characters (the limit of WordPress).

label and singularLabel must not be empty; slug consists of lowercase letters, digits, - and _.

The class is not abstract and declares no properties.

custom-fields is added to supports when the type has meta; without it the REST API has no meta.

#[PostMeta(name, title, postTypes)] on a meta class:

The class is not readonly and not abstract; all its fields are promoted constructor parameters (any visibility), each with a default.

name: lowercase letters, digits and _, starting with a letter, unique per post type.

postTypes: a non-empty list of post classes (#[PostType], Post, Page).

Meta keys {namespace}_{field_in_snake_case} are unique per post type.

A field on the top level may be nullable only if it holds an ID – a reference or an image: the REST API of WordPress leaves out meta fields whose type allows null. A missing ID is stored as no value (0 in the REST API).

#[Content] and #[RichText(postContent: true)] are build errors; allowedFormats may only contain the formats of the classic editor.

#[Taxonomy(name, label, singularLabel, postTypes, labels: null, description: '', public: true, showUi: true, hierarchical: false, slug: null)] on a class without constructor parameters: the full name has at most 32 characters.

A class has at most one of #[PostType], #[PostMeta] and #[Taxonomy]. Post, meta and taxonomy classes cannot be injected and are no parameters of hooks, REST routes or template functions.

Most classes in a theme or plugin need other classes: a hook needs a formatter, a block needs the template renderer, a REST route needs a repository. You don't create these objects yourself, and you don't register them anywhere. You ask for them in the constructor, and the framework's container passes them in.

final class Formatter
{
    public function format(string $title, int $postId): string { /* … */ }
}

final class TitleFilter
{
    public function __construct(private readonly Formatter $formatter) {}

    #[Hook('the_title')]
    public function filter(string $title, int $postId = 0): string
    {
        return $this->formatter->format($title, $postId);
    }
}

TitleFilter asks for a Formatter, so the container creates one and passes it in. Formatter needs no registration: it is a class in the theme's src/, which makes it a service.

Which classes are services

Every concrete class under the consumer's PSR-4 directories (autoload.psr-4 in its composer.json) and under the framework's src/ is a service candidate. These are not:

interfaces, abstract classes, traits and enums;

attribute classes;

classes marked with #[NoService] (Saij\WpAppFramework\DependencyInjection\NoService);

settings groups. A constructor parameter typed with a settings group receives the group with its stored values instead;

post, meta and taxonomy classes: they are data. Load posts with their class, Event::get($id);

classes with #[MetaFields]: their static methods provide fields for queries;

row classes (#[Table]) and classes with #[TableFields]: rows are data. Load them with their class, LogEntry::get($id).

The container wires services by the types of their constructor parameters. A parameter with a scalar type (string, int …) cannot be filled from the container and must have a default value. There is exactly one instance of each service per request; every class that asks for it gets the same object.

Only what is used is built

You don't have to mark every class that is not meant as a service. The build starts from the entry points and follows their constructor parameters. Entry points are:

methods with #[Hook], #[RestRoute], #[CliSubcommand], #[TemplateFunction], #[TemplateFilter], #[CronEvent] or #[CronTask], including the permission callbacks of REST routes;

classes with #[Block].

Only classes reachable from there are built and checked. A value object that no constructor asks for is simply ignored. A parameter of a reachable class that cannot be filled is a build error naming the class and parameter; two classes that need each other are a build error naming the chain of classes.

Services are created lazily: when the hook fires, the route is called, the WP-CLI command runs or the block renders – not when WordPress loads.

The container stays internal

The container is internal: your code cannot inject it or ask it for a service. Aliases and tags are not supported. Your code receives services only through constructor parameters, and in hooks and REST routes also through method parameters (see Hooks and REST routes). This keeps the dependencies of each class visible in its signature, and it lets the build check them.

Predefined services

A few services are provided by the framework rather than built from a class of yours.

The project object describes the theme or plugin itself and gives you its paths and URLs. Which class it is depends on the kind of project:

Project	Class	Methods
Theme	Saij\WpAppFramework\Theme	getTemplateDirectory(), getTemplateDirectoryUri(), getStylesheetDirectory(), getStylesheetDirectoryUri(), getFrameworkDirectory()
Plugin	Saij\WpAppFramework\Plugin	getSlug(), getFile(), getDirectory(), getDirectoryUri(), getFrameworkDirectory()
Child theme	Saij\WpAppFramework\ChildTheme	getSlug(), getParentSlug(), getDirectory(), getDirectoryUri(), getParentDirectory(), getParentDirectoryUri(), getFrameworkDirectory()

The boot method creates the object (its constructor is private) and hands it to the container, which never builds it itself. Injecting the class of another kind – Plugin in a theme, for example – is a build error.

Saij\WpAppFramework\Twig\TemplateRenderer is predefined as well. The framework creates it on first use (see Twig templates).

Block markup is styled with Tailwind CSS 4. The work is split between the framework and the theme. The framework compiles the stylesheet and loads it on the front end and in the editor. The design – which Tailwind tokens exist and which WordPress values they point to – belongs to the theme.

The framework ships no stylesheet for the front end of its own, because design tokens differ from theme to theme.

Setting up the stylesheet

A theme, child theme or plugin that has a stylesheet assets/styles.css gets it compiled to build/styles.css by the webpack helper. The stylesheet lives in assets/, the directory for everything that belongs to no PHP class (see Concepts). Only this file goes through Tailwind, using Tailwind's official loader @tailwindcss/webpack. All other stylesheets of the build keep the PostCSS setup of @wordpress/scripts. A consumer without assets/styles.css doesn't need Tailwind at all.

Install Tailwind next to @wordpress/scripts:

npm install --save-dev tailwindcss@4 @tailwindcss/webpack@4

If the packages are missing, the build fails with a message that names them. The helper looks for assets/styles.css when it starts, so restart npm run start after adding or removing the file.

Here is a stylesheet for a theme:

@layer theme, base, components, utilities;
@import "tailwindcss/theme.css" layer(theme);
@import "tailwindcss/utilities.css" layer(utilities) source(none);

/* Where the class names are: the Twig templates of the theme and its overrides. */
@source "../src/**/*.twig";
@source "../overrides/**/*.twig";

/* Tokens on the values of theme.json. */
@theme inline {
	--color-primary: var(--wp--preset--color--primary);
	--color-contrast: var(--wp--preset--color--contrast);
	--spacing-section: var(--wp--preset--spacing--50);
	--container-content: var(--wp--style--global--content-size);
	--container-wide: var(--wp--style--global--wide-size);
}

The following sections go through it.

Tokens

theme.json is where a block theme defines its colors, font sizes and spacing. WordPress turns these into CSS variables such as --wp--preset--color--{slug}, --wp--preset--font-size--{slug}, --wp--preset--spacing--{slug} and --wp--style--global--content-size. Users can change many of these values in the site editor.

@theme inline maps Tailwind tokens to these variables, so bg-primary becomes background-color: var(--wp--preset--color--primary). The keyword inline makes the utility refer to the WordPress variable itself rather than to a copy. When someone changes a color in the backend, only the variable changes, and the stylesheet never needs a rebuild.

To allow only your own tokens, reset Tailwind's defaults, for example with --color-*: initial;.

Sources

Tailwind generates only the classes it finds in your files. source(none) turns off its automatic detection, and @source lists the files to scan, relative to the stylesheet.

Name the Twig files explicitly. Scanning PHP files would produce classes from words in the code, such as static, block, hidden or container. A word in a template that is not meant as a utility can be excluded with @source not inline("container") – for example a class container of the theme's own, which Tailwind would otherwise style as its container utility.

Templates in overrides/ are rendered with the theme's stylesheet, so they belong to its sources.

Class names

Write class names out in full in the templates. Never assemble them from attribute values, as in gap-{{ gap }}: Tailwind cannot find gap-4 in that template, so the class would be missing. Map values to complete class names instead:

{% set gaps = { small: 'gap-2', large: 'gap-8' } %}
<div class="flex {{ gaps[attrs.gap.value] }}">…</div>

Class names generated at run time, such as those of block supports, can be listed with @source inline(…).

Preflight

The example does not import Preflight, Tailwind's reset (tailwindcss/preflight.css). It would reset the styles of core blocks on the front end and in the editor canvas. Import it deliberately if the theme styles everything itself.

Loading

If the consumer has assets/styles.css, the build records it in the manifest. The framework then loads build/styles.css on enqueue_block_assets with the handle {slug}-styles. That reaches the front end and the canvas iframes of the post and site editor, but not the main document of the editor, so the admin interface stays unstyled. add_editor_style() is not needed.

A missing build/styles.css is an exception naming the file. Fonts the page needs early can be marked for preloading in their @font-face (see Preloading fonts). A stylesheet in its former place, src/styles.css, is a build error, both in the manifest build and in the webpack helper.

The styles-rtl.css that @wordpress/scripts writes is not used. Tailwind handles the writing direction itself, with logical properties (ms-4, ps-4) and the rtl: variant.

Editor and front end

The editor canvas renders a block from the same server markup as the front end and loads the same stylesheet. Every element of a block gets the same size, spacing and type in the canvas as on the page, so content editors see the real spacing.

The framework's canvas styles help with one difference: WordPress lets text in blocks break anywhere in the editor, and the canvas styles restore the wrapping of the front end. They live in the cascade layer base, so your utilities still win.

Some things a theme has to do itself, and some stay different on purpose.

Space between blocks. Set settings.spacing.blockGap in theme.json, with a value in styles.spacing.blockGap. Without it, WordPress 7.1 puts its default gap between blocks in the editor, but none on the front end.

Width of blocks. settings.layout.contentSize in theme.json is optional. Without it, WordPress still constrains blocks to the undefined content size in the editor – which means no maximum width, the whole canvas – but not on the front end. A maximum width set with a utility on the block's root element then applies on the front end only. Set a content size if blocks need the same width in both places.

WordPress' layout wins. Tailwind's utilities live in cascade layers, WordPress' layout styles do not, and styles outside layers win regardless of specificity. The width of a block in a constrained layout (max-width, centering margins) and the space between blocks therefore come from theme.json, not from utilities on the block's root element. Use utilities to style the inside of a block.

Empty fields are rendered in the editor, with a placeholder, so they can be filled in. On the front end the template leaves them out ({% if attrs.title or is_editor() %}).

White space. Fields edited in the canvas keep typed spaces and line breaks visible (white-space: pre-wrap, set by WordPress' RichText); the front end collapses them.

The page around the blocks is WordPress' editor. In the post editor without the template, the content is not inside .wp-site-blocks, and the root padding sits on the canvas body.

Plugins and child themes

Class names are global, and every instance builds its own stylesheet. If a plugin used the same class names as the theme with different values, whichever stylesheet loads last would win.

A plugin therefore uses a prefix of lowercase letters a–z on both imports, so its classes and variables never meet those of the theme:

@import "tailwindcss/theme.css" layer(theme) prefix(note);
@import "tailwindcss/utilities.css" layer(utilities) source(none) prefix(note);

<div class="note:p-4 note:bg-accent">…</div>

Tailwind's internal variables (--tw-*, registered with @property) carry no prefix. Every Tailwind 4 stylesheet on the page shares them.

A child theme with code shares the design of its parent theme and uses the same tokens without a prefix.

A child theme without code builds no stylesheet. Its overrides use classes that the parent theme's stylesheet already contains, or plain CSS in its style.css. If it needs further utilities, it becomes a child theme with code and gets a build of its own (see Child themes).

wp-app-framework is a PHP and JavaScript framework for building standalone full site editing themes, child themes and plugins for WordPress 7 on PHP 8.5. You write blocks, hooks, REST routes and settings as plain PHP classes with attributes. The framework turns them into everything WordPress expects: registered blocks with an attribute schema, an editor with inline editing, REST routes with validation, and a settings page.

The framework is a library, not a theme or a plugin. Your theme, child theme or plugin includes it through Composer; the documentation calls these projects consumers. The framework registers no blocks itself; the blocks, hooks and routes you see in WordPress come from your code.

Each consumer runs its own copy of the framework, called an instance. Its blocks, REST routes and settings are named after the consumer's namespace from composer.json; technical names – script handles, the JavaScript global, the cache directory, the init action – after its slug (its directory name). That is why a theme and several plugins can run side by side, even with different framework versions. Concepts explains this and the other ideas the guides build on.

Principles

The framework follows a few rules that shape every part of its API. Knowing them makes many details in the guides easier to follow.

No markup in the post content. Every block renders on the server. The post content stores only a block comment with the attributes as JSON, for example <!-- wp:my-theme/teaser {"title":"Summer"} /-->; block supports are attributes too. You can change a block's markup at any time, and every post shows the new markup without being saved again. The one exception is a single #[RichText(postContent: true)] field per block, which keeps the text of paragraphs and headings in the post content (see Blocks).

Inline editing in the editor canvas. Content editors type directly into the block, as with core blocks. The markup still exists only once, in a Twig template: the editor renders the block on the server and replaces the marked elements with editors (see Editor).

Full site editing. Pages are made of block templates and template parts, as in any block theme. Twig renders the markup of blocks and fragments that your code produces, such as mails or HTML responses – never whole page layouts.

Plain WordPress. A release ships all dependencies, including the framework and Twig, prefixed with the consumer's own namespace. The server needs neither Composer nor Bedrock, and in a release two consumers never load each other's classes (see Release).

Compiled in the build, not at run time. A build step reads attributes such as #[Hook], #[RestRoute] and #[Block] and writes a manifest of plain data. Mistakes such as a missing permission or a service that cannot be created show up as build errors, and a release runs without reflection (see Boot and modes).

Status

The framework is in development (0.x). Minor versions may still contain breaking changes; Upgrading lists them and how to adapt your code.

These parts are ready to use:

the framework core: boot, service container, manifest build, development mode and #[Hook];

REST routes with #[RestController], #[RestRoute] and #[Param];

Twig templates with TemplateRenderer, #[TemplateFunction] and #[TemplateFilter];

server-side blocks with #[Block], #[Supports], typed attribute classes, #[ListOf] (also for REST arguments) and references to posts, terms and users;

block supports of your own with #[BlockSupport], #[UseSupport] and registerSupportControl();

settings with #[SettingsGroup], one option per group, a settings page, injection into services and the template global settings;

post types, taxonomies and typed post meta with #[PostType], #[Taxonomy] and #[PostMeta]: post objects that load and save themselves, panels in the block editor and meta boxes on the classic edit screen;

queries of post objects with Event::query(): conditions on meta fields and terms, groups, sorting, pages and caching, with field classes (#[MetaFields]) written by hand or by wp-app-framework meta-fields;

tables of your own with #[Table]: row objects that load and save themselves, queries with LogEntry::query() and field classes (#[TableFields], wp-app-framework table-fields), created and kept up to date by the framework;

the editor runtime: inline editing in the canvas, a generated inspector, toolbar buttons, a batch render route and a webpack helper;

front-end scripts of blocks and block supports with the base classes BlockView and SupportView, loaded only with their blocks and previewed in the editor;

the release build wp-app-framework release (prefixed ZIP), release mode with a versioned Twig cache, and GitHub workflows;

plugins: Plugin::boot(), theme overrides of plugin templates, public APIs and public template callables;

child themes: ChildTheme::boot(), overrides of theme templates in overrides/{theme}/, public APIs and template callables of themes, and #[ThemeApi].

Where to start

If you are new to the framework, read Concepts first. Then follow Getting started, which sets up a theme with a first block. The guides then explain each part of the API in turn. Values describes the typed values that blocks, REST routes and settings share.

The test theme theme-dev-test contains a working example of every feature. It comes with the test plugin plugin-dev-test and the test child theme theme-dev-test-child. wp-dev-env provides a local WordPress for development.

